    "title": "Caltrans cancels closure of 'last SF exit' on 80 this weekend",
    "text": "The Fourth Street offramp in San Francisco is fully closing for four days as Caltrans works on the viaduct.Google Street ViewLATEST April 8, 8:30 a.m. In response to weather, Caltrans announced that it was postponing the planned closure of the Fourth Street exit on eastbound Interstate 80 for a later date.“Crews were expected to place polyester concrete on the freeway. Because this material is highly sensitive to moisture and rain is forecast throughout the weekend, the closure was necessary,” the agency wrote in a statement.Article continues below this adApril 8, 4 a.m. The last San Francisco exit when approaching the Bay Bridge is closing for the weekend as part of Caltrans’ ongoing rehabilitation of crucial viaducts near downtown, where Interstate 80 intersects with Highway 101. From late Friday, April 10, through early Monday, April 13, drivers on eastbound I-80 attempting to enter the SoMa neighborhood from the freeway will only have the Seventh Street exit. Caltrans is closing the Fourth Street offramp, dubbed the “last SF exit” on the nearby highway sign.If drivers miss the earlier exit, Caltrans advises them to drive onto Yerba Buena Island to boomerang back to reach SoMa via the Fifth Street offramp on westbound 80.Make SFGATE a preferred source so your search results prioritize writing by actual people, not AI.Add Preferred Source“It is standard Caltrans practice to show detour routes after the closed exit to assist motorists who missed the advance signs,” Caltrans spokesperson Lori Shepherd told SFGATE in email. “The next exit for the 4th Street exit is some distance away on Yerba Buena Island.”Article continues below this adThe weekend closure will be disruptive for motorists driving in and around the work area, and Caltrans is urging motorists to drive cautiously and consider public transportation. Caltrans encourages motorists who must drive to use the detour map.Caltrans is urging motorists to drive cautiously near the work area, located where Interstate 80 intersects with Highway 101 in San Francisco.Courtesy of CaltransCaltrans started the two-year process to rehabilitate the 71-year-old viaducts in January. The closures this month address the I-80 Bayshore Freeway viaducts. Work on the Central Freeway viaducts is expected to begin in November.Got a tip? Send us the scoop.Got a tip? Send us the scoop.DO IT NOW Crews are placing polyester overlays and joints on the viaducts while repairing barrier rails and the bridge’s deck. Caltrans is instituting a series of closures this month to address maintenance. The agency is also prepping for a second closure during the following weekend, from April 17 through the 20. Caltrans plans to close a section of eastbound I-80 between 17th Street and Fourth Street for 55 hours, opening a detour on Bryant Street.Article continues below this adThe maintenance continues Caltrans’ focus on safeguarding these active viaducts that run through San Francisco. Several years ago, Caltrans repainted the steel beneath the viaducts with a vivid blue color that has an unofficial tie to a nearby sports dynasty. Shepherd said the coating protects the steel by using a formula to prevent fading. Not only is the paint the same blue that’s seen on the Coronado Bridge in San Diego and other bridges in California, it’s a subtle complement to the Golden State Warriors who play close by.“Coincidentally, the Warriors moved into the Chase for the 2019/2020 season,” Shepherd said. “And the blue pretty much matched the Warriors colors [when] the project started in 2020.”April 8, 2026|Updated April 8, 2026 8:35 a.m.Silas Valentino is SFGATE’s travel editor. He was born in Bakersfield and raised in Marin County. Outside of reading, writing and storytelling, Silas values his family (including 11 nieces and nephews) and exploring the state. He lives with his wife and son above a wine shop in Cole Valley. You can contact him at silas.valentino@sfgate.com.",
